WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.648
This lesson becomes much more useful when power is read as a rate.

2
00:00:05.648 --> 00:00:11.296
Power tells how quickly energy is transferred or converted.

3
00:00:11.296 --> 00:00:16.944
A high-power device is not just a big device.

4
00:00:16.944 --> 00:00:22.592
It is a device transferring energy rapidly.

5
00:00:22.592 --> 00:00:28.240
Once that meaning is secure, the energy formulas and safety ideas become much easier to connect, because wire heating, fuse choice, and electrical load all depend on how much current and power the circuit is dealing with.

6
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.888
Keep this takeaway in view: Power is a rate, and that rate connects directly to energy use and safety.

7
00:00:33.888 --> 00:00:39.536
Power means rate.

8
00:00:39.536 --> 00:00:45.184
Power tells how quickly energy is transferred.

9
00:00:45.184 --> 00:00:50.832
Energy means total transferred.

10
00:00:50.832 --> 00:00:56.480
Energy depends on how powerful the device is and how long it runs.

11
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:02.884
The circuit diagram is a reminder that electrical formulas are describing real hardware.

12
00:01:02.884 --> 00:01:09.288
Current through a wire can cause heating.

13
00:01:09.288 --> 00:01:15.691
A fuse is chosen so it melts and breaks the circuit if the current becomes unsafe.

14
00:01:15.691 --> 00:01:22.095
An appliance rating tells how quickly it transfers energy in normal operation.

15
00:01:22.095 --> 00:01:28.499
The diagram matters because it ties together the abstract equations with the physical safety system around them.

16
00:01:28.499 --> 00:01:34.902
Keep this takeaway in view: Power and current calculations should always stay connected to the safety story.

17
00:01:34.902 --> 00:01:41.306
Appliance rating: Power tells the rate at which the device transfers energy.

18
00:01:41.306 --> 00:01:47.710
Current in wires: Higher current increases heating and safety demands.

19
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:52.379
Imagine water flowing into a bucket.

20
00:01:52.379 --> 00:01:57.048
The amount of water arriving each second is like power: a rate.

21
00:01:57.048 --> 00:02:01.717
The total water collected after some time is like energy transferred.

22
00:02:01.717 --> 00:02:06.386
A faster tap fills the bucket more quickly, just as a higher-power appliance transfers energy more quickly.

23
00:02:06.386 --> 00:02:11.055
And even a modest tap can fill a lot of water if it runs for long enough.

24
00:02:11.055 --> 00:02:15.725
That is why energy depends on both power and time.

25
00:02:15.725 --> 00:02:20.394
Keep this takeaway in view: Power is the transfer rate, and energy is the total accumulated transfer.

26
00:02:20.394 --> 00:02:25.063
Per second flow means power.

27
00:02:25.063 --> 00:02:29.732
Power describes how quickly the transfer happens.

28
00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:34.401
Total collected means energy.

29
00:02:34.401 --> 00:02:39.070
Energy is the total amount transferred after time passes.

30
00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:43.372
Three relations matter here.

31
00:02:43.372 --> 00:02:47.674
Power equals current times voltage.

32
00:02:47.674 --> 00:02:51.976
Energy equals power times time.

33
00:02:51.976 --> 00:02:56.279
And by combining them, energy can also be written as voltage times current times time.

34
00:02:56.279 --> 00:03:00.581
These formulas fit together because they describe the same circuit behavior at different levels.

35
00:03:00.581 --> 00:03:04.883
Power tells the rate.

36
00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:09.185
Energy tells the total after time.

37
00:03:09.185 --> 00:03:13.487
Voltage and current tell the electrical conditions producing that rate.

38
00:03:13.487 --> 00:03:17.789
Keep this takeaway in view: These formulas are one connected system, not three separate facts.

39
00:03:17.789 --> 00:03:22.091
Power: Voltage and current together set the transfer rate.

40
00:03:22.091 --> 00:03:26.394
Energy: Running for longer increases the total transferred energy.

41
00:03:26.394 --> 00:03:30.696
P = IV.

42
00:03:30.696 --> 00:03:34.998
E = Pt.

43
00:03:34.998 --> 00:03:39.300
Use watts for power, joules for energy, and seconds for time.

44
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.526
A high-power appliance transfers energy more quickly than a low-power one.

45
00:03:44.526 --> 00:03:49.753
If both run for the same time, the higher-power device transfers more energy.

46
00:03:49.753 --> 00:03:54.979
If the voltage is fixed, higher power also means higher current, because P equals I times V.

47
00:03:54.979 --> 00:04:00.205
That is why powerful devices place greater demands on wires and protection.

48
00:04:00.205 --> 00:04:05.432
The lesson is not only about doing multiplication.

49
00:04:05.432 --> 00:04:10.658
It is about understanding what a power rating says about real circuit behavior.

50
00:04:10.658 --> 00:04:15.885
Keep this takeaway in view: Power ratings describe both energy use and current demand.

51
00:04:15.885 --> 00:04:21.111
Same time means more power -> more energy.

52
00:04:21.111 --> 00:04:26.337
A higher transfer rate builds a larger total in the same interval.

53
00:04:26.337 --> 00:04:31.564
Same voltage means more power -> more current.

54
00:04:31.564 --> 00:04:36.790
The device must draw more current if the voltage stays fixed.

55
00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:43.600
A fuse should be rated just above the normal operating current so it can allow normal use but still break the circuit if the current becomes dangerously large.

56
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:50.410
If a device draws more current than the wire or fuse system can safely handle, overheating can happen.

57
00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:57.220
This is why current calculations matter beyond the worksheet.

58
00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:04.030
The safe design of a circuit depends on predicting realistic current and power demands accurately.

59
00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:10.840
Keep this takeaway in view: Electrical safety depends on understanding current and power, not guessing them.

60
00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:17.650
Normal current should stay below the fuse rating.

61
00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:24.460
A fuse is a safety device, not a power booster.

62
00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.088
Try three checks.

63
00:05:28.088 --> 00:05:31.717
What does power mean?

64
00:05:31.717 --> 00:05:35.345
The rate of energy transfer.

65
00:05:35.345 --> 00:05:38.974
What happens to total energy if the same power runs for longer?

66
00:05:38.974 --> 00:05:42.602
The total energy increases.

67
00:05:42.602 --> 00:05:46.231
And at the same voltage, what must happen to current if power increases?

68
00:05:46.231 --> 00:05:49.859
The current must increase as well.

69
00:05:49.859 --> 00:05:53.488
Those three ideas are the backbone of the lesson and the safety section too.

70
00:05:53.488 --> 00:05:57.116
Keep this takeaway in view: The lesson is stable when rate, total energy, and current demand all connect.

71
00:05:57.116 --> 00:06:00.745
Meaning means rate of transfer.

72
00:06:00.745 --> 00:06:04.373
Power tells how fast energy is being transferred.

73
00:06:04.373 --> 00:06:08.002
Longer time means more energy.

74
00:06:08.002 --> 00:06:11.630
Energy increases when the same power acts for longer.

75
00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:18.072
You are ready for the worked example now.

76
00:06:18.072 --> 00:06:24.513
If a device runs at 12 volts and 2 amperes, use P equals I times V to get the power.

77
00:06:24.513 --> 00:06:30.955
Twelve times two gives 24 watts, so the device transfers energy at 24 joules per second.

78
00:06:30.955 --> 00:06:37.397
If it runs for 10 seconds, use E equals P times t.

79
00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:43.838
Twenty-four times ten gives 240 joules.

80
00:06:43.838 --> 00:06:50.280
Keep the meaning attached all the way through: power is the rate, energy is the total after time, and the current value also matters for safe fuse and wiring choices.

81
00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:56.722
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: calculate clearly, and keep the physical meaning attached.

82
00:06:56.722 --> 00:07:03.163
Step 1: Use voltage and current to calculate the power.

83
00:07:03.163 --> 00:07:09.605
Step 2: Use the power and the time to calculate the total energy transferred.

84
00:07:09.605 --> 00:07:16.047
P = IV = 12 x 2 = 24 W.

85
00:07:16.047 --> 00:07:22.488
E = Pt = 24 x 10 = 240 J.

86
00:07:22.488 --> 00:07:28.930
Read 24 W as 24 joules transferred each second.
